{"parentUuid":"ae63b5ba-50fc-4bae-a6e6-99a785d960d1","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"505f5b2a-6f54-4004-9ada-8e02163a6b4a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TrdwLZoDwSDzxpQvDwv1Cj","type":"tool_result","content":"=== ashtom | internal/sem/search_prose_parent.go:45 | 2026-08-17T03:13:05Z\n### Section units leak into code results, and silently override `--max-regions-per-file`\n\nOnce the prose gate opens, `proseParentKey` keys by `SymbolID:StartLine` for **every** candidate — and the gate only requires ≥80% of matched *paths* to be doc extensions, so the source files in a docs-heavy repo come along with it. The prose selector never consults `maxPerFile` except via `baseline`.\n\nMeasured on a repo of 20 `.md` + 3 `.go` files, `--top-k 30`:\n\n| | results | regions of `pkg/file00.go` |\n|---|---|---|\n| `main` | 23 | 1 |\n| this PR (default) | 31 | **7** |\n\n`MaxRegionsPerFile` defaults to 3. Under document resolution the one-unit-per-file rule made that cap moot; section units remove the rule, and nothing reinstates the cap.\n\n**Why the PR's own check misses it:** the \"code search is unaffected — 30 of 30 payloads identical\" verification ran against this Go repo, whose path mix fails the ≥80% gate, so it never reaches this code at all. A mixed docs+code fixture would.\n\nSuggested direction: consult `maxPerFile` in the prose selector, or restrict section keying to candidates whose own path is a doc extension rather than relying on the repo-level gate.\n\n---\n<sub>🤖 Agent-generated review finding. Posted from @ashtom's account, but written by Claude Code, not by a human reviewer — weigh it accordingly. Every measurement above was produced by building this branch in a throwaway worktree and running probes against synthetic corpora, comparing to `main`.</sub>\n\n=== ashtom | internal/sem/search_multi_resolution.go:126 | 2026-08-17T03:13:06Z\n### Promoted results break descending-score ordering of `results[]`\n\n`applyProseResolutionPromotions` appends promotions after each parent, while `proseResolutionResult` inherits `parent.Score` verbatim. The result array is therefore no longer monotone in score.\n\nMeasured on 6 docs × 10 turns at `--top-k 200`: **rank 66 carries score `1.6592`, rank 67 (a promoted passage) carries `15.9715`.**\n\nEvery pre-PR payload I probed was monotone (`--top-k` 20/50/100 all clean), so this is new. It matters because it breaks precisely the consumer the PR description reasons about: anything that sorts or truncates by score now sees a ranking that contradicts its own rank numbering — a client taking \"top 20 by score\" gets a different set than the first 20 entries.\n\nTwo ways out, depending on intent: give promotions a score derived from their own match rather than the parent's, or state in the schema that `results[]` is rank-ordered and *not* score-ordered, so consumers stop trusting the score for ordering.\n\n---\n<sub>🤖 Agent-generated review finding. Posted from @ashtom's account, but written by Claude Code, not by a human reviewer — weigh it accordingly. Every measurement above was produced by building this branch in a throwaway worktree and running probes against synthetic corpora, comparing to `main`.</sub>\n\n=== ashtom | internal/sem/search_prose_parent.go:137 | 2026-08-17T03:13:07Z\n### Section results nested inside a higher-ranked same-file window reprint the same text\n\nProse head ranks get an 80-line head window (`resolvedSearchHeadWindowLines`), and section units now place several results from one file in a single payload. Lower-ranked results can therefore fall entirely inside a higher-ranked result's printed snippet.\n\nMeasured on 8 docs × 12 turns at `--top-k 20`: rank 1 prints `sessions/session-00.md` lines **1–63**; ranks 9, 10, 11 and 12 return the same file at lines **8, 13, 18, 23** — all already visible in rank 1. At `--top-k 100`: **59 overlapping same-file result pairs out of 100 results.**\n\nSince the payload is budgeted and replayed into a model on every later turn, those are bytes spent twice.\n\n**Why the tests don't catch it:** `TestSearchRepositoryReturnsEveryProseRegionOnce` compares exact `(SnippetStartLine, SnippetEndLine)` tuples, so containment is invisible to it — distinct tuples pass even when one span sits inside another. And `dropSelectedProsePassages` dedupes against pre-growth spans, never re-checking after `allocateSearchSnippets` grows them.\n\n---\n<sub>🤖 Agent-generated review finding. Posted from @ashtom's account, but written by Claude Code, not by a human reviewer — weigh it accordingly. Every measurement above was produced by building this branch in a throwaway worktree and running probes against synthetic corpora, comparing to `main`.</sub>\n\n=== ashtom | internal/sem/search_multi_resolution.go:135 | 2026-08-17T03:13:09Z\n### The promotion fitter re-serializes the whole result set once per candidate count\n\n```go\nfor count := 1; count <= len(order); count++ {\n    trial := applyProseResolutionPromotions(...)\n    serializedSearchResultBytes(trial)\n}\n```\n\nEach iteration rebuilds and JSON-marshals up to `topK` results. Benchmarked at 19 parents × 12 passages, `--top-k 200`, 4 MB budget: **25.6 ms and 39 MB allocated per call**, 181 serializations.\n\nSerialized size is strictly monotone in `count` — a promotion always moves a passage into a strictly larger result record — so a binary search finds the same fit in ~8 serializations.\n\nSeparately: when `budget <= 0` the serialization short-circuits, but the loop still performs `len(order)` full slice copies where a single call with `count = len(order)` would do.\n\n---\n<sub>🤖 Agent-generated review finding. Posted from @ashtom's account, but written by Claude Code, not by a human reviewer — weigh it accordingly. Every measurement above was produced by building this branch in a throwaway worktree and running probes against synthetic corpora, comparing to `main`.</sub>\n\n=== ashtom | internal/sem/search.go:187 | 2026-08-17T03:13:10Z\n### `SingleResolution`'s doc comment states a contract the code does not honour\n\nThe comment says the flag restores \"the previous output exactly: one result per file\".\n\nMeasured with 8 docs at `--top-k 40`, `SingleResolution: true`: **40 results over 8 files**, with `sessions/session-00.md` supplying 10 of them. Only `SingleResolution && DocumentResolution` together reproduce the old shape — 23 results, byte-matching `main` on the mixed corpus.\n\nThis one is worth fixing even though it is only a comment: AGENTS.md names entire-brain as a downstream consumer of this package, and a caller reading this comment to restore prior behaviour sets one flag and gets something else. Either correct the comment to name both flags, or make `SingleResolution` imply document resolution so the comment becomes true.\n\n---\n<sub>🤖 Agent-generated review finding. Posted from @ashtom's account, but written by Claude Code, not by a human reviewer — weigh it accordingly. Every measurement above was produced by building this branch in a throwaway worktree and running probes against synthetic corpora, comparing to `main`.</sub>\n\n=== ashtom | internal/sem/search_prose_session_test.go:849 | 2026-08-17T03:13:11Z\n### The scale guard now covers only the non-default path\n\n`TestSelectSearchCandidatesHasLinearProseWorkingSet` (line 846) and `BenchmarkSelectSearchCandidatesProse4000` (line 923) were both updated to pass `sectionUnits=false` — and `proseScaleCandidates` emits one candidate per file, so section units would be a no-op there even if the flag were flipped.\n\nThat leaves the **default** path unpinned, and it is the more expensive one: a second full `proseParents` pass, a per-section `planProseParentPassages` call over the whole document's candidate list, and a term-list key space that grows with sections rather than files.\n\nMeasured at 200 files × 20 sections, `--top-k 20`:\n\n| | allocs | bytes |\n|---|---|---|\n| before | 2,486 | 2.0 MB |\n| after | **23,219** | 4.4 MB |\n\nA guard that only covers the path the product does not take will not notice the next regression. Worth a fixture with real sections so the default path has a ceiling.\n\n---\n<sub>🤖 Agent-generated review finding. Posted from @ashtom's account, but written by Claude Code, not by a human reviewer — weigh it accordingly. Every measurement above was produced by building this branch in a throwaway worktree and running probes against synthetic corpora, comparing to `main`.</sub>\n\n=== ashtom | internal/cli/help.go:130 | 2026-08-17T03:13:12Z\n### The help text presents the two levers as alternatives when they stack\n\nThe wording reads: *\"Prose with no heading structure has nothing to split on; there, when fewer distinct units match than --top-k asked for, the spare slots are spent returning finer regions\"* — which says multi-resolution is what happens **instead of** section splitting, for unheaded prose.\n\nMeasured: headed markdown with section resolution active at `--top-k 200` still produced **33 results tagged `retrieval_mode=prose-passage`**. Multi-resolution runs on headed prose too.\n\nThat is not just a wording nit — it is the same stacking that produces the score-ordering break reported on `search_multi_resolution.go`. A reader who believes the two are alternatives will not expect promoted passages in a headed-prose payload at all.\n\n---\n<sub>🤖 Agent-generated review finding. Posted from @ashtom's account, but written by Claude Code, not by a human reviewer — weigh it accordingly. Every measurement above was produced by building this branch in a throwaway worktree and running probes against synthetic corpora, comparing to `main`.</sub>","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"b55908fe-596a-45c4-8873-6760d7867875","timestamp":"2026-08-17T03:20:15.340Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=== ashtom | internal/sem/search_prose_parent.go:45 | 2026-08-17T03:13:05Z\n### Section units leak into code results, and silently override `--max-regions-per-file`\n