{"parentUuid":"569ea99d-5a0e-4143-a920-41b52e91536e","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"505f5b2a-6f54-4004-9ada-8e02163a6b4a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q2jgzocgsrV4MrSwGDf65x","type":"tool_result","content":"180\t\t// It used to be switched on IMPLICITLY by `TopK > 10`, which made every observable\n181\t\t// property of a search discontinuous at that boundary: the git-tree-grep preselector\n182\t\t// was disabled, every file in the repo was read, the indexed-file pool shrank, region\n183\t\t// deduplication stopped and the reported score stopped being a relevance score. Asking\n184\t\t// for one more result is not a request to change retrieval strategy, so the strategy is\n185\t\t// now an explicit choice and top-k only changes how many rows come back.\n186\t\tDeep bool\n187\t\t// SingleResolution turns OFF multi-resolution prose retrieval, restoring the previous output\n188\t\t// exactly: one result per file, with the finer regions of a prose document left in each\n189\t\t// result's `passages` field instead of being promoted to results of their own. See\n190\t\t// search_multi_resolution.go — the expansion is strictly additive (it only fills result slots\n191\t\t// the distinct-file ranking left empty, and never breaches MaxContextBytes), so this switch\n192\t\t// exists for callers that need the result count to equal the distinct-file count, not because\n193\t\t// the expansion can cost them a file.\n194\t\tSingleResolution bool\n195\t\t// DocumentResolution turns OFF section-resolution prose ranking, restoring the previous\n196\t\t// behaviour: one prose document is one retrievable unit, so a corpus of N documents can never\n197\t\t// return more than N ranked regions however large --top-k is. With it unset (the default), a\n198\t\t// prose document's SECTIONS are the ranked units — each competes on its own score exactly as\n199\t\t// an independent file would — which is what every comparable document retriever does and what\n200\t\t// a reader asking a question about a long document actually wants. See search_prose_parent.go.\n201\t\tDocumentResolution bool\n202\t}\n203\t\n204\t// SearchPassage is an additional, non-overlapping source region from the same file as its\n205\t// parent result. It lets prose retrieval return two distant answer-bearing turns without\n206\t// pretending the unrelated text between them was one contiguous snippet.\n207\ttype SearchPassage struct {\n208\t\tStartLine int    `json:\"start_line\"`\n209\t\tEndLine   int    `json:\"end_line\"`"}]},"uuid":"b427a4f2-5f55-4dc0-ac38-ac676d182ec0","timestamp":"2026-08-17T03:22:09.165Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/eg-multires/internal/sem/search.go","content":"\t// It used to be switched on IMPLICITLY by `TopK > 10`, which made every observable\n\t// property of a search discontinuous at that boundary: the git-tree-grep preselector\n\t// was disabled, every file in the repo was read, the indexed-file pool shrank, region\n\t// deduplication stopped and the reported score stopped being a relevance score. Asking\n\t// for one more result is not a request to change retrieval strategy, so the strategy is\n\t// now an explicit choice and top-k only changes how many rows come back.\n\tDeep bool\n\t// SingleResolution turns OFF multi-resolution prose retrieval, restoring the previous output\n\t// exactly: one result per file, with the finer regions of a prose document left in each\n\t// result's `passages` field instead of being promoted to results of their own. See\n\t// search_multi_resolution.go — the expansion is strictly additive (it only fills result slots\n\t// the distinct-file ranking left empty, and never breaches MaxContextBytes), so this switch\n\t// exists for callers that need the result count to equal the distinct-file count, not because\n\t// the expansion can cost them a file.\n\tSingleResolution bool\n\t// DocumentResolution turns OFF section-resolution prose ranking, restoring the previous\n\t// behaviour: one prose document is one retrievable unit, so a corpus of N documents can never\n\t// return more than N ranked regions however large --top-k is. With it unset (the default), a\n\t// prose document's SECTIONS are the ranked units — each competes on its own score exactly as\n\t// an independent file would — which is what every comparable document retriever does and what\n\t// a reader asking a question about a long document actually wants. See search_prose_parent.go.\n\tDocumentResolution bool\n}\n\n// SearchPassage is an additional, non-overlapping source region from the same file as its\n// parent result. It lets prose retrieval return two distant answer-bearing turns without\n// pretending the unrelated text between them was one contiguous snippet.\ntype SearchPassage struct {\n\tStartLine int    `json:\"start_line\"`\n\tEndLine   int    `json:\"end_line\"`","numLines":30,"startLine":180,"totalLines":5072}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"569ea99d-5a0e-4143-a920-41b52e91536e","session_id":"fe41fb4b-5221-41ce-afce-6a9fa3dade0c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/eg-multires","sessionId":"27642e0f-a981-4143-aea0-6f4f8cb34d37","version":"2.1.226","gitBranch":"main","slug":"structured-meandering-origami"}
